==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|gratious}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʷerH-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|gracious}}, from {{der|en|fro|gracieus}}, from {{der|en|la|gratiosus}}, from {{m|la|gratia||esteem, favor}}. See {{m|en|grace}}. Displaced native {{cog|en|hold||gracious}}. {{doublet|en|gracioso|grazioso}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɡɹeɪʃəs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-gracious.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃəs|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of, related to, [[full]] of, or [[exhibit#Verb|exhibit]]ing [[grace#Noun|grace]].
#: {{nearsyn|en|graceful<qq:overlaps>}}
## [[kind#Adjective|Kind]] and [[warmly]] [[courteous]].
## [[compassionate|Compassionate]].
## [[indulgent|Indulgent]].
## [[benignant|Benignant]].
## [[benevolent|Benevolent]].
## [[charming|Charming]].
## [[elegant|Elegant]] {{q|in appearance, conduct, movement}}.
##: {{ux|en|The '''gracious''' movements of the figure skaters impressed the judges.}}
## [[magnanimous|Magnanimous]], without [[arrogance]] or [[complaint]], benevolently declining to raise controversy or insist on possible prerogatives.
##: {{ux|en|The actress's '''gracious''' acceptance of being named only in the end credits allowed her character's appearance in the episode to remain a surprise.}}
## [[tactful|Tactful]].<!--shares with the [[magnanimous]] sense a component of advisedly forgoing critique-->
## {{lb|en|religious}} Full of or exhibiting the grace of God {{n-g|(which can entail most of the preceding senses)}}.
##: {{nearsyn|en|blessed#Adjective}}
##: {{coi|en|a '''gracious''' plenty}}

